.TH PS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ps \- list the processes running on the system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
.RB [ \-AadeflZ ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR columns ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ps
writes a table of the running processes on standard output: a header
line, then one line per process.
It takes a single snapshot and exits.
It lists every process on the system, including those of other users and
system processes such as
.BR System ,
and has no way to select a subset.
Processes appear in the order Windows lists them, not sorted by process
ID.
.PP
The default columns are
.BR pid ,
.BR ppid ,
.BR user ,
.BR time ,
.BR etime ,
and
.BR args .
.B \-o
chooses others from the eight described under
.BR Columns .
.SS Output
Each column is padded with spaces to its width and followed by a space,
except the last, which is not padded.
.B pid
and
.B ppid
are right-aligned within their width, and the other columns start at the
left edge of theirs.
A value wider than its column is cut to the width, except for
.B pid
and
.BR ppid ,
which are written in full and push the rest of the line to the right.
An empty value is written as
.BR \- .
.PP
The header line uses the same widths, with every header at the left edge
of its column, including those of
.B pid
and
.BR ppid .
It is omitted when every column has an empty header, as with
.BR "\-o pid=,comm=" .
.PP
Each line is cut to a limit: one column less than the width of the
console when standard output is a console, up to 2048, and 2048
otherwise.
Columns after the first one that, with its trailing space, reaches past
the limit are not printed, even when their values would fit.
As
.B args
is 2048 wide, a column after it is printed only when
.B args
has a header that narrows it.
Lines end in LF, and
.B ps
writes no escape sequences.
.SS Columns
.TP
.B pid
The process ID.
Header
.BR PID ,
width 5.
.TP
.B ppid
The ID of the parent process.
Header
.BR PPID ,
width 5.
It is 1 when the parent is not running, because Windows does not
reparent a process whose parent has exited, and 0 for process 0, which
has no parent.
Windows can reuse the ID of an exited parent, and the value is then the
ID of an unrelated process.
.TP
.B user
The user name.
Header
.BR USER ,
width 8.
It is the name of the current user for a process that
.B ps
can open with full access, and
.B root
for any other process.
It is not the name of the process's owner; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B group
The group name, which is the same as the user name.
Header
.BR GROUP ,
width 8.
.TP
.B comm
The name of the process: the file name of its executable, including the
extension, such as
.BR explorer.exe ,
cut to 31 bytes.
A process running the same build of busybox is named after its applet
instead, such as
.B sh
or
.BR sleep .
Header
.BR COMMAND ,
width 32.
.TP
.B args
The command line.
For a process running the same build of busybox, including
.B ps
itself, it is the applet name and the arguments it was given, joined by
single spaces with no quoting.
At most 126 characters are kept, and an argument that does not fit is
left out together with all those after it.
For any other process
.B ps
does not read the command line, and the value is the same as
.BR comm .
Header
.BR COMMAND ,
width 2048.
.TP
.B time
The CPU time the process has used, user and kernel together, truncated
to whole seconds.
Header
.BR TIME ,
width 5.
The value is always five characters, padded on the left with spaces, in
the first of these forms that fits:
minutes and seconds, as in
.BR 3:07 ;
hours and minutes, as in
.BR 2h05 ;
days and hours, as in
.BR 12d03 ;
or, from 100 days, days alone, as in
.BR 100d .
.TP
.B etime
The time since the process started, in the same forms as
.BR time .
Header
.BR ELAPSED ,
width 7.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-Aef ,
and the argument of
.B \-o
may be attached, as in
.BR \-opid,args .
Options may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Operands are ignored, so
.B ps aux
is the same as
.BR ps .
Any other option, such as
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-w ,
or
.BR \-h ,
prints an error message and a usage message on standard error and exits
with status 1.
.TP
.BI "\-o " columns
Show the columns named in
.IR columns ,
in that order, instead of the default ones.
.I columns
is a list of names from
.BR Columns ,
separated by commas.
The names are lower case, and blanks are not separators.
The option may be repeated, and the lists are joined in order.
A name unknown to
.B ps
is an error: it prints the names it knows and exits with status 1.
.IP
A name may be followed by
.BI = header
to replace that column's header with
.IR header .
The header ends at the next comma, so it cannot contain one.
.IP
A header also sets the width of its column.
The column is exactly as wide as the header, even when that is narrower
than the default width, and values longer than that are cut off, except
for
.B pid
and
.BR ppid .
For example,
.B \-o comm=NAME
shows names of at most four characters.
Giving
.B pid
or
.B ppid
a header narrower than its values makes
.B ps
overrun its line buffer, which can corrupt the output.
An empty header, as in
.BR \-o\ pid= ,
keeps the default width and shows no header text.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-a ", " \-d ", " \-e ", " \-f ", " \-l ", " \-Z
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and exit
with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width of the console, in place of its real width, when standard
output is a console.
A value that is not from 2 to 29999 is taken as 80.
It has no effect when standard output is not a console.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When it is set, even to an empty value, the first operand ends the
options, so that in
.BR "ps aux \-o pid" ,
.B \-o
is an operand and is ignored.
.PP
The variables that busybox reads for every applet, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a missing argument to
.BR \-o ,
or an unknown column name.
.PP
Failure to list the processes is not detected: if Windows refuses to
supply the list, only the header is printed and the status is 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the processes with the default columns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ps
PID   PPID  USER     TIME  ELAPSED COMMAND
    0     0 root      0:00  1d05   [System Process]
    4     0 root      0:00  1d05   System
\&...
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the distinct program names that are running, with no header:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ps \-o comm= | sort \-u
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find a background job and its parent, which is the shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sleep 60 &
[1] 11732
$ ps \-o pid,ppid,etime,comm | grep \-w sleep
11732 13472  0:01   sleep
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show how a header sets the column width, here cutting names to four
characters:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ps \-o pid,comm=NAME | head \-3
PID   NAME
    0 [Sys
    4 Syst
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the procps
.B ps
and from busybox
.B ps
on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no way to select processes or change the format other than
.BR \-o .
The POSIX options
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-l ,
and the BSD form
.BR "ps aux" ,
are accepted and ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-w ,
or other option, and no column for the terminal, state, memory use,
process group, session, or niceness.
.IP \(bu 2
The default columns include
.B ppid
and
.BR etime ,
which busybox on Linux leaves out.
.IP \(bu 2
Process names include the extension, such as
.BR cmd.exe .
.IP \(bu 2
.B ps
does not look up the owner of a process, so
.B user
and
.B group
are not the owner's name; see below.
.PP
The list comes from
.BR CreateToolhelp32Snapshot ,
so a process that exits while
.B ps
runs is still listed.
Whether a process is shown as the current user is decided by trying to
open it with
.BR PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS .
The current user's name comes from
.BR GetUserName ,
with each space replaced by an underscore, and is cut to the width of
the column.
Processes that
.B ps
cannot open that way, such as those of other users and protected system
processes, are shown as
.BR root .
.PP
CPU and start times come from
.BR GetProcessTimes ,
which needs only
.BR PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION ,
so it can succeed for a process shown as
.BR root .
For a process whose times cannot be read,
.B time
is
.B 0:00
and
.B etime
is the time the system has been up.
The times are kept as 32-bit counts of milliseconds, so
.B time
wraps after about 49 days of CPU time, and
.B etime
is wrong for a process that started more than about 49 days after the
system booted.
.PP
The name and command line of a process other than
.B ps
itself are read out of the memory of that process.
That works only for a process that runs the same build of busybox as
.B ps
and whose memory
.B ps
can open for reading.
Any other process is shown under its executable's file name for both
.B comm
and
.BR args .
.PP
The kit's command launchers, such as
.BR ps.exe ,
appear under their own names, each alongside the busybox process it
started.
When
.B ps
is started through its launcher, as from
.BR cmd.exe ,
the output has a row for
.B ps.exe
and another for
.BR ps .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR killall (1),
.BR pgrep (1),
.BR pidof (1),
.BR pkill (1)
